1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,833
41 projects.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,033
30 judges.

3
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:05,333
126 reviews.

4
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:06,900
One winner.

5
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:09,800
But can you prove they deserved to win?

6
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,600
This is Dogfood Judge. Results you can defend.

7
00:00:16,133 --> 00:00:19,600
Judge A loves everything. Nines and tens.

8
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:23,067
Judge B is tough. Fives and sixes.

9
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,967
Judge C gives almost everyone a seven.

10
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,233
The usual approach: average the scores, sort, declare a winner.

11
00:00:30,733 --> 00:00:36,400
But judges don't use the scale the same way. Draw the harsh judge, and you lose on luck.

12
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:41,667
What if one review changed the winner? What if removing one judge flips it?

13
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:44,967
I built Dogfood Judge to answer those questions.

14
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:51,767
It's a complete, self-hosted hackathon platform: Go, SQLite, and Next.js.

15
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:57,900
Events, teams, submissions, assignments, scoring, analysis, publishing, and verification.

16
00:00:58,233 --> 00:01:00,633
But the real work happens after scoring.

17
00:01:01,767 --> 00:01:05,900
Assignments respect tracks, conflicts of interest, and load balance.

18
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:09,733
Now, what if one judge asks for another judge's scores?

19
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:13,100
The backend refuses. 403.

20
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:20,733
Isolation is enforced by the server, not hidden by the UI. A 44-case authorization matrix proves it.

21
00:01:21,967 --> 00:01:23,500
Now, the judging engine.

22
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:29,867
A raw score mixes two things: how good the project is, and how generous its judges were.

23
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:35,367
Dogfood models each judge's leniency and scale, using the overlap between judges.

24
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:40,467
Corrections are shrunk with empirical Bayes, so sparse judges aren't over-corrected.

25
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:46,933
And every rank comes with error bars: a rank interval, and the probability of a top three finish.

26
00:01:48,167 --> 00:01:51,200
Which brings us to the question every losing team asks.

27
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,867
Is the winner defensible?

28
00:01:53,467 --> 00:01:58,733
Dogfood removes each judge in turn and refits the whole model. 30 refits.

29
00:01:59,133 --> 00:02:06,100
On the sample event, first place survives 24 of 30. Six specific judges can change the winner.

30
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:11,067
The lead over second place? 0.05 standard errors.

31
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:18,133
Other platforms print first place and move on. Dogfood says it's a coin flip, and how to settle it.

32
00:02:19,567 --> 00:02:22,133
Next, reviews that disagree with everyone.

33
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:26,967
The model predicts every review. Reviews it can't explain get flagged.

34
00:02:27,267 --> 00:02:32,400
In our test suite, one planted rogue review moved a project from #19 to #29.

35
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:37,300
It doesn't just flag a review. It measures what that review cost.

36
00:02:38,533 --> 00:02:42,367
When the podium is close, reviewing everything again is waste.

37
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:47,400
Most projects are already settled, near 0% or near 100%.

38
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:52,567
The tie-breaker round finds the coin flips at the prize line, and adds a judge to each.

39
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:56,800
Judge time goes where another review can actually change the outcome.

40
00:02:58,033 --> 00:03:02,367
Sometimes a score isn't enough. So there's a pairwise mode.

41
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:06,067
A judge sees two projects. A, B, or tie.

42
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:10,267
The server picks the pairs adaptively, so no judge can steer them.

43
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:15,733
Verdicts are fitted with Bradley–Terry, a regularizing prior, and real standard errors.

44
00:03:16,967 --> 00:03:20,600
Fairness is half the story. The other half is trust.

45
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,667
Publishing produces a signed bundle: pseudonymized inputs, a fingerprint, an audit anchor, and the ranking.

46
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,300
Anyone can run dogfood verify-results. It re-runs the exact engine, offline.

47
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:34,367
Verified.

48
00:03:34,967 --> 00:03:37,833
Now change a single score, and run it again.

49
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:42,100
Verification failed. The fingerprint no longer matches.

50
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,033
Judges can even verify their own reviews in the browser, with Merkle proofs.

51
00:03:48,267 --> 00:03:50,233
And it's all tested end to end.

52
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:56,133
The Go suite boots the real portal. Real SQLite, real HTTP, not mocks.

53
00:03:56,467 --> 00:04:02,033
The organizers' checker passes T1 and T2. Ours covers T3 and T4.

54
00:04:02,367 --> 00:04:10,567
Authorization, deadlines, conflicts of interest, tamper detection, CSRF, CSV injection, rate limits, and signed webhooks.

55
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:14,100
And it all runs on your own machine.

56
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:17,067
One command. docker compose up.

57
00:04:17,467 --> 00:04:23,200
After the first build, pull the network. It keeps running, with zero outbound requests.

58
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:26,600
So let's go back to that leaderboard.

59
00:04:26,900 --> 00:04:29,333
This time, the winner comes with evidence.

60
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:34,133
Picking a winner is easy.

61
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:37,000
Proving the result is fair is harder.

62
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:42,967
I'm Prince Panchani. This is Dogfood Judge. Results you can defend.
